xUnit has two attributes you need on day one.

`[Fact]` is a test with no parameters — one scenario, asserted once:

```csharp
public class TicketPolicyTests
{
    [Fact]
    public void Owner_can_edit_an_open_ticket()
    {
        var decision = TicketPolicy.AuthorizeEdit(
            new Agent(7, "agent"),
            new Ticket { Id = 42, OwnerId = 7, Status = "open" });

        Assert.Equal(EditDecision.Allow, decision);
    }
}
```

`[Theory]` is the same test run over several inputs, each supplied by an `[InlineData]`:

```csharp
[Theory]
[InlineData(0, false)]
[InlineData(1, true)]
[InlineData(5, true)]
[InlineData(6, false)]
public void Page_size_is_accepted_only_within_range(int size, bool expected)
{
    Assert.Equal(expected, Paging.IsAcceptable(size));
}
```

Those four rows are not arbitrary. The rule is 1 to 5 inclusive, so the interesting values are the ones **either side of each edge**: 0 and 1 at the bottom, 5 and 6 at the top. That set catches `<` written where `<=` was meant, in both directions. Testing 3 proves almost nothing — no plausible mistake makes 3 fail.

That is the whole of boundary-case selection, and it is worth stating as a habit: **for each edge in the rule, test the last value that passes and the first that fails.**

Two structural things differ from Vitest and will bite once each.

xUnit creates **a new instance of the test class for every test method**. Fields initialised in the constructor are therefore fresh per test — which is good isolation, and means a field cannot be used to share setup cost. When setup is genuinely expensive, that is what `IClassFixture<T>` is for: one instance shared across the class, with the sharing made explicit rather than accidental.

And there is no `[SetUp]`/`[TearDown]`. The constructor is setup; implementing `IDisposable` is teardown.

```csharp
public class TicketQueryTests : IDisposable
{
    private readonly SupportContext _db;

    public TicketQueryTests() => _db = TestContext.Create();   // per test

    public void Dispose() => _db.Dispose();
}
```

Finally, the failure this lesson shares with V1 chapter 12: a test that restates the implementation. If the production code computes `total * 1.2m` and the test asserts `total * 1.2m`, the test agrees with the code by construction and will keep agreeing after the rate changes, after a typo, after anything. Assert the **expected value** — `Assert.Equal(120m, ...)` — because a literal is the only thing that can disagree with the code.

items.workedExampleMarkdown:
The same rule, tested badly and then well.

The production code:

```csharp
public static class Paging
{
    public const int MaxPageSize = 50;

    public static int Clamp(int requested) =>
        requested < 1 ? 1 : requested > MaxPageSize ? MaxPageSize : requested;
}
```

**A test that cannot fail:**

```csharp
[Theory]
[InlineData(0)]
[InlineData(75)]
public void Clamp_matches_the_rule(int requested)
{
    var expected = requested < 1 ? 1 : requested > Paging.MaxPageSize ? Paging.MaxPageSize : requested;
    Assert.Equal(expected, Paging.Clamp(requested));
}
```

The expectation is the implementation, pasted. Change `>` to `>=` in both places and this still passes. It also silently follows `MaxPageSize` wherever it goes, so a wrong constant is invisible.

**A test that can:**

```csharp
[Theory]
[InlineData(0, 1)]      // below the floor
[InlineData(1, 1)]      // the floor itself
[InlineData(25, 25)]    // untouched in the middle
[InlineData(50, 50)]    // the ceiling itself
[InlineData(51, 50)]    // just above the ceiling
[InlineData(-4, 1)]     // well below
public void Clamp_holds_the_boundaries(int requested, int expected)
{
    Assert.Equal(expected, Paging.Clamp(requested));
}
```

Every expectation is a literal, so the test knows the answer independently of the code. The pairs 0/1 and 50/51 pin both edges: swapping either comparison to its non-strict form now fails a row. And `[InlineData(50, 50)]` is not redundant with `[InlineData(51, 50)]` — one asserts the ceiling is *included*, the other that it is *enforced*, and a single off-by-one distinguishes them.

What this does not test is more interesting than what it does: it says nothing about routing, model binding or whether the endpoint even calls `Clamp`. That is the next lesson's job.

solution.code:
function boundaryValues(range) {
  if (range.max < range.min) return [];
  const candidates = [range.min - 1, range.min, range.max, range.max + 1];
  const unique = Array.from(new Set(candidates));
  return unique.sort((a, b) => a - b);
}

grading.starterCode:
function boundaryValues(range) {
  // Both sides of both edges, deduplicated and in numeric order.
  return [range.min, range.max];
}

