A unit test of a handler calls a method. A request to your API does considerably more: it is routed, its body is bound and validated, it passes through authentication and authorization middleware, the result is serialised, and a status code is chosen. Every one of those steps is a place defects live, and none of them is exercised by calling the method directly.

`WebApplicationFactory<TProgram>` starts your actual application in memory and hands you an `HttpClient` that talks to it:

```csharp
public class TicketApiTests : IClassFixture<WebApplicationFactory<Program>>
{
    private readonly HttpClient _client;

    public TicketApiTests(WebApplicationFactory<Program> factory) =>
        _client = factory.CreateClient();

    [Fact]
    public async Task Unknown_ticket_returns_404()
    {
        var response = await _client.GetAsync("/tickets/999999");
        Assert.Equal(HttpStatusCode.NotFound, response.StatusCode);
    }
}
```

No port is bound and no network is used; the request goes through the real pipeline in-process. That is what makes these tests fast enough to run constantly while still being honest.

The defects they catch are exactly the ones unit tests structurally cannot see: a route that never matched, a model binder that silently left a property at its default, an `[Authorize]` that was never applied, a 500 that should have been a 400, a serialised property name the client does not expect, and middleware registered in the wrong order — the bug from D08 that makes every authenticated request look anonymous.

Customising the host is done by overriding services:

```csharp
public class TestFactory : WebApplicationFactory<Program>
{
    protected override void ConfigureWebHost(IWebHostBuilder builder)
    {
        builder.ConfigureTestServices(services =>
        {
            services.RemoveAll<DbContextOptions<SupportContext>>();
            services.AddDbContext<SupportContext>(o =>
                o.UseNpgsql(TestDatabase.ConnectionString));

            services.AddAuthentication("Test")
                    .AddScheme<AuthenticationSchemeOptions, TestAuthHandler>("Test", _ => { });
        });
    }
}
```

And here is where these tests are most often quietly ruined. **Every substitution removes something from the test.** Replacing the database with an in-memory provider removes the constraints, the transactions, and the SQL translation — which is to say, most of what chapter D06 and D07 were about. Replacing authentication with a handler that always succeeds removes the authorization checks, so the D08 ownership defect passes cleanly.

That does not make substitution wrong. The test-auth handler above is a reasonable trade: it lets a hundred tests avoid a real login while a handful of dedicated tests exercise the genuine scheme. What matters is doing it deliberately and knowing what you gave up. The one substitution not worth making is the database: point at a real, disposable PostgreSQL instance. The V1 platform's own suites do this, and it is the reason its foreign keys, unique constraints and transaction boundaries are actually tested rather than assumed.

One caution that this project learned expensively. Integration tests share process-level state — the database especially. Two suites truncating the same tables will produce failures that look precisely like logic bugs. Give each suite its own database, or run them strictly sequentially, and make that arrangement explicit rather than hoping.

